minorities. Some key informants saw these local power and social dynamics as a barrier to achieving an equitable form of localization (Interviews 2, 10, 25). However, key respondents also noted it was important to avoid using these dynamics as an excuse for saying, “it’s complicated,” and avoiding localization commitments. Rather, it is important that localization efforts pay attention to and respect the diversity of all crisis-affected communities.
